===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# {{lb|en|Hawaii}} [[black nightshade]] ({{taxfmt|Solanum nigrum|species}}).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1883|title=All about Hawaii|author=|page=36|ISBN=|passage=The only lack of Kalelealuaka and his comrades was animal food (literally fish) but they supplied its place aw well as they could with such herbs as the tender leaves of the '''popolo''', which they cooked like spinach, and with inamona made from the roasted nuts of the kukui tree.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1975|title=Practical folk medicine of Hawaii|author=L. R. McBride|page=67|ISBN=|passage='''Popolo''' is a branching green herb with a tendency to being woody at the base. This annual grows one to three feet high on cultivated land and is regarded as a common weed.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|title=Proceedings of the Hawaiian Entomological Society|author=Hawaiian Entomological Society|page=|ISBN=|passage=This mite was collected at the Maui Community College agricultural field (Kahului) on '''popolo''' berry on 19 July 1995 by C. McGrath.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2011|title=Hawaiian Legends of Ghosts and Ghost-Gods|author=William D. Westervelt|page=|ISBN=1462901360|passage=He turned and went back at the call of the messenger, taking some '''popolo''' fruit and leaves in his cloak.}}
# {{lb|en|Hawaii|slang|countable|sometimes pejorative}} A [[black]] person.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2004|title=Social Process in Hawaii - Volume 43|author=Miles M. Jackson|page=92|ISBN=|passage=Well, I'm a Hawaiian, but my father was a black-assed '''popolo'''—a Portuguese from Bravas, Cabo Verde Islands, who changed his name.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007|title=Little League, Big Dreams|author=Charles Euchner|page=|ISBN=1402248342|passage=When he gets to third, Fe'ao is excited. He sees Curaçao's thir baseman, Denjerick Virginie, starting at him as he slaps his hands together. "What you looking at, '''popolo'''?" he barks.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|title=A Tale of Two Brothers|author=Dave Burgess|page=176|ISBN=0557560950|passage=I think that most people in Hawaii agree with my mother-in-law's view of Barry as a good Hawaiian boy; a '''popolo''' Hawaiian boy, but a Hawaiian boy nonetheless.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2014|title=Redefining Realness: My Path to Womanhood, Identity, Love & So Much More|author=Janet Mock|page=|ISBN=1476709130|passage=There was a racial order that existed even in this group of tweens. They teased that Chad and I were '''popolo''', Hawaiian slang for black people. Popolo are shiny berries that grow in clusters in the islands and are so black that they shine purple on branches.}}

